# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites every text
# file to CRLF on checkout. For a file that only Windows reads that is
# harmless. For a file a *Linux container* runs it is not: the shell reads the
# trailing \r as part of the last word, so `set -euo pipefail` becomes
# `set -euo pipefail\r` and the script dies on line 3 with
# "set: pipefail: invalid option name".
#
# That is not a theoretical failure. `scripts/init_localstack.sh` is mounted
# into the LocalStack container as a ready hook; when it dies that way it
# creates none of the six buckets, and the first presigned upload answers
# `NoSuchBucket` -- which looks like a backend bug and is a checkout artefact.
#
# So: anything executed inside a container, or by Git Bash, is LF in the
# working tree regardless of platform.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts want CRLF for the same reason, in reverse.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Everything else: normalise to LF in the repository, let Git decide the
# working tree.
*       text=auto

# Fonts bundled with the resume builder. Binary, never normalised: one
# rewritten byte and a glyph table no longer parses.
*.ttf   binary
